{"name":"Aqueduct","hex":"#60b3bc","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aqueduct","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An artificial channel that is constructed to convey water from one location to another.","A structure carrying water over a river or depression, especially an ancient structure."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aqueduct"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aqueduct (bridge)","blocks":[{"text":"Aqueducts are bridges constructed to convey watercourses across gaps such as valleys or ravines. The term aqueduct may also be used to refer to the entire watercourse, as well as the bridge. Large navigable aqueducts are used as transport links for boats or ships. Aqueducts must span a crossing at the same level as the watercourses on each end. The word is derived from Latin aquaeductus from aqua (\"water\") and ducere, therefore meaning \"water-leader\". A modern version of an aqueduct is a pipeline bridge. They may take the form of tunnels, networks of surface channels and canals, covered clay pipes or monumental bridges."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Acueducto%2C_Arcos_del_Sitio%2C_Tepotzotl%C3%A1n.jpg/330px-Acueducto%2C_Arcos_del_Sitio%2C_Tepotzotl%C3%A1n.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aqueduct_(bridge)","wikidata":"Q18870689"},{"title":"Navigable aqueduct","blocks":[{"text":"Navigable aqueducts are bridge structures that carry navigable waterway canals over other rivers, valleys, railways or roads. They are primarily distinguished by their size. They carry a larger cross-section of water than most water-supply aqueducts. Roman aqueducts were created in ancient Rome. They were used to transport water. The 662-metre (2,172 ft) long steel Briare aqueduct carrying the Canal latéral à la Loire over the River Loire was built in 1896. It was ranked as the longest navigable aqueduct in the world for more than a century. In the early 21st century, the Magdeburg Water Bridge in Germany was constructed, which became the new longest navigable aqueduct in the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Pontcysyllte_aqueduct_arp.jpg/330px-Pontcysyllte_aqueduct_arp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navigable_aqueduct","wikidata":"Q1030403"},{"title":"Aqueduct (water supply)","blocks":[{"text":"An aqueduct is a watercourse constructed to carry water from a source to a distribution point far away. In modern engineering, the term aqueduct is used for any system of pipes, ditches, canals, tunnels, and other structures used for this purpose. The term aqueduct also often refers specifically to a bridge carrying an artificial watercourse."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Pont_du_Gard_BLS.jpg/330px-Pont_du_Gard_BLS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aqueduct_(water_supply)","wikidata":"Q474"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Vanvitelli_aqueduct.jpg/330px-Vanvitelli_aqueduct.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Vanvitelli_aqueduct.jpg/330px-Vanvitelli_aqueduct.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aqueduct of Vanvitelli, Italy, built by Luigi Vanvitelli. It is a World Heritage Site.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVanvitelli_aqueduct.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Aqueduct_of_Segovia_02.jpg/500px-Aqueduct_of_Segovia_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Aqueduct_of_Segovia_02.jpg/1280px-Aqueduct_of_Segovia_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Aqueduct_of_Segovia_02.jpg/500px-Aqueduct_of_Segovia_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aqueduct of Segovia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAqueduct_of_Segovia_02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Aquadukt_Kavala_GREECE.jpg/500px-Aquadukt_Kavala_GREECE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Aquadukt_Kavala_GREECE.jpg/500px-Aquadukt_Kavala_GREECE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kavala aqueduct, Greece","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAquadukt_Kavala_GREECE.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Vila_do_Conde_3.jpg/500px-Vila_do_Conde_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Vila_do_Conde_3.jpg/1280px-Vila_do_Conde_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Vila_do_Conde_3.jpg/500px-Vila_do_Conde_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An aqueduct in Vila do Conde, Portugal","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVila_do_Conde_3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Tomar_December_2008-4.jpg/500px-Tomar_December_2008-4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Tomar_December_2008-4.jpg/1280px-Tomar_December_2008-4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Tomar_December_2008-4.jpg/500